// case: member-call-without-lowering
// what: `xs.sort()` named a member the builder has no branch for. The front
//      end typed the call `unknown`, the builder emitted `sort(xs, ...)` and
//      the C step met an implicit declaration of a function nobody wrote --
//      the same failure n110 pins for the bare call, reached through a
//      receiver whose type was known all along.
//
//      A member call is not a function call: the member set of a receiver the
//      checker enumerates is the set the builder lowers, and a name outside it
//      is refused before any C is emitted.
// expect-error: 'list<int>' has no member 'sort'.
fn main() -> int {
    var xs = [3, 1, 2]
    xs.sort()
    return 0
}
